      "note": "Archive only, and DELIBERATELY A SLICE. DIFFERENT DATASET: EQUS.MINI does not carry `statistics` at all (measured 2026-09-07 \u2014 it answers 422 dataset_schema_not_supported); EQUS.SUMMARY does, its history begins 2024-07-01, and it quotes $0.00 inside the Standard plan. THE SIZE IS NOT WHAT THIS ENTRY USED TO CLAIM. It read `symbolSets: [BASELINE, BENCHMARKS], start: 2024-07-01` and described itself as \"halts, auctions and session statistics\" \u2014 the smallest entry in the manifest. Measured against metadata.get_record_count on 2026-09-08, that specification is 13,270,557,774 records and 790.99 GiB billable: 92% of the ENTIRE harvest, larger than every other entry combined, for an entry with `load: false` and no `consumer`. Narrowing does not rescue it either \u2014 TOP50 over a trailing 12 months still measures 28,106,92,777 records and 167.53 GiB, because the statistics volume is concentrated in exactly the busiest names. That is ~2.2 million records per symbol per trading day, which is not a halt-and-auction feed by any reading. So what is bought here is an INSPECTION SLICE: four benchmark ETFs, one recent month, roughly 3 GiB. Look at what the schema actually contains, then widen deliberately with the numbers above in hand. Widening is a founder decision and a one-line manifest edit; it is deliberately not the default, because the default here was a three-quarter-terabyte download nobody had priced.",
